IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `poa-dates`
IMPORT `poa-core`

-- Powers of Attorney Act 2003 (NSW) -- the second seat: the principal's family member, named beneficiary or other
-- interested person.
--
-- SEAT: a person close to the principal who is not the principal: a beneficiary named in a deceased principal's will
-- whose gift the attorney sold or dealt with (ss 22, 23), or a relative, guardian or friend who wants a tribunal to look
-- at the power (ss 35, 36). Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Do I keep my share in what replaced
-- the property the attorney sold?", "Is that money real or personal estate?", "By when must I apply to the Supreme
-- Court?", "Is my application in time?", "Can I apply for a review?", "Can the tribunal declare the power invalid, or
-- remove the attorney?". It draws on poa-core.l4 for the shared review rule, so the two seats cannot disagree.
--
-- ANSWERS as in poa-core.l4: JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the Act's words say no; NOTHING (with a NOTHING-kind comment)
-- where the Act does not answer.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 2 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Powers of Attorney Act 2003 -- the family member, beneficiary or interested person`

§§ `The nouns`

-- s 22(2)-(4): what was received from the attorney's dealing.
DECLARE `What came from the attorney's dealing` IS ONE OF
    `surplus money or property from selling or dealing with real estate`
    `surplus money or property from selling or dealing with personal property`
    `money for equality of partition or exchange of real estate`
    `a fine, premium or sum received on granting or renewing a lease of real estate`
    `such a sum, where the deceased was only tenant for life of the leased property`

DECLARE `Real or personal estate` IS ONE OF `real estate`, `personal estate`

-- The party and act of the s 23(3) rule.
DECLARE `Applicant party` IS ONE OF `the applicant`
DECLARE `Applicant act` IS ONE OF `apply to the Supreme Court under s 23`

§§ `s 22 -- when the attorney sold or dealt with property left to me (a situation: the principal has died)`

-- s 22(1) "Any person who is named as a beneficiary ... under the will of a deceased principal who executed an enduring
-- power of attorney has the same interest in any surplus money or other property arising from any sale, mortgage,
-- charge or disposition of any property or other dealing with property by the attorney under the power of attorney as
-- the named beneficiary would have had in the property ... if no sale ... had been made." (5) subject to a s 23 order.
-- (6) "A person is named as a beneficiary ... if (a) the person is referred to by name in the will as being a
-- beneficiary, or (b) the person answers a description of a beneficiary, or belongs to a class of persons specified as
-- beneficiaries". (7) "This section does not apply to any person to whom section 83 of the NSW Trustee and Guardian Act
-- 2009 applies" (a borrowed test, asked).
@export My late relative's attorney sold or dealt with property their will left to me: do I have the same interest in what is left over from it (s 22)?
GIVEN `the will names me as a beneficiary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will refers to you by name as a beneficiary
      `I answer a description or class of beneficiaries in the will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you answer a description of a beneficiary, or belong to a class of beneficiaries, in the will (for example "my grandchildren")
      `the deceased made an enduring power of attorney` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died had made an enduring power of attorney
      `the attorney dealt with the property under that power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the attorney sold, mortgaged, charged, disposed of or otherwise dealt with the property under that enduring power
      `money or property is left over from the dealing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any surplus money or other property arising from that dealing remains
      `section 83 of the NSW Trustee and Guardian Act applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether s 83 of the NSW Trustee and Guardian Act 2009 applies to you (it covers beneficiaries where the estate was under financial management)
      `the Supreme Court has made a section 23 order about this` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order under s 23 about this property or its proceeds
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do I keep my interest in what is left from the attorney's dealing` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `section 83 of the NSW Trustee and Guardian Act applies to me` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 22(7) "does not apply"
           IF (NOT `the will names me as a beneficiary`) AND (NOT `I answer a description or class of beneficiaries in the will`) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 22(6) "if"
           IF (NOT `the deceased made an enduring power of attorney`) OR (NOT `the attorney dealt with the property under that power`) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 22 speaks only of dealings under an enduring power; ademption under the general law decides others)
           IF (NOT `money or property is left over from the dealing`) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 22 is silent where nothing is left over)
           IF `the Supreme Court has made a section 23 order about this` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the Act: the order decides, s 22(5))
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE  -- s 22(1)

-- s 22(2) the surplus "is taken to be of the same nature as the property sold"; (3) "Except as provided by subsection (4),
-- money received for equality of partition and exchange, and all fines, premiums and sums of money received on the grant
-- or renewal of a lease where the property ... was real estate of a deceased principal are to be considered as real
-- estate"; (4) where the deceased principal was tenant for life, "personal estate".
@export Is the money or property from the attorney's dealing treated as real estate or personal estate in my late relative's estate (s 22(2)-(4))?
GIVEN `what came from the dealing` IS A `What came from the attorney's dealing` @desc What the money or property is. One of: "surplus money or property from selling or dealing with real estate", "surplus money or property from selling or dealing with personal property", "money for equality of partition or exchange of real estate", "a fine, premium or sum received on granting or renewing a lease of real estate", "such a sum, where the deceased was only tenant for life of the leased property"
GIVETH A `Real or personal estate`
`Is it real estate or personal estate` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what came from the dealing`
    WHEN `surplus money or property from selling or dealing with personal property` THEN `personal estate`  -- s 22(2)
    WHEN `such a sum, where the deceased was only tenant for life of the leased property` THEN `personal estate`  -- s 22(4)
    OTHERWISE `real estate`  -- s 22(2), (3)

§§ `s 23 -- applying to the Supreme Court about it (a situation: the principal has died)`

-- s 23(3) "An application under subsection (1) must be made within 6 months from the date of the grant or resealing in
-- this State of probate of the will or letters of administration unless the Supreme Court ... extends the time"; (4)(b)
-- an extension may be granted "whether or not the time ... has expired". Fork F9 for the end day.
GIVEN `the date of the grant` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to apply under s 23` `the date of the grant` MEANS `the date of the grant` `plus months` 6

@export By what day must I apply to the Supreme Court under s 23 about property the attorney dealt with, unless the Court extends the time (s 23(3))?
GIVEN `the day probate or administration was granted or resealed in NSW` IS A DATE @desc The day probate of the will, or letters of administration, was granted or resealed in New South Wales
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must I apply to the Supreme Court under s 23` MEANS
    `the last day to apply under s 23` `the day probate or administration was granted or resealed in NSW`

-- Written as an obligation on whoever applies: the application must be made within the six months, time 0 being the day
-- of the grant, in days. An application before the grant is counted at day 0 (fork F10).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant party` `Applicant act`
`s 23(3) -- the application must be made within 6 months from the grant` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `apply to the Supreme Court under s 23`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "the s 23 application was not made within 6 months from the grant or resealing of probate or administration, and the Supreme Court has not extended the time (s 23(3))"

-- s 23(1) "On the application of a named beneficiary referred to in section 22 (1) or such other person as the Supreme
-- Court considers has a proper interest in the matter". Someone who is neither cannot apply (fork F11).
@export Can I make my s 23 application to the Supreme Court, on the day I plan to, in time (s 23(1), (3), (4))?
GIVEN `I am a named beneficiary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are named in the will as a beneficiary, by name or by description or class (s 22(6))
      `the Court considers I have a proper interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are not a named beneficiary, whether the Supreme Court considers you have a proper interest in the matter
      `the day probate or administration was granted or resealed in NSW` IS A DATE @desc The day probate of the will, or letters of administration, was granted or resealed in New South Wales
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or plan to file) your application
      `the Court has extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has extended the time for your application (it can do so even after the six months have passed)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my s 23 application in time` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I am a named beneficiary`) AND (NOT `the Court considers I have a proper interest`) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 23(1), fork F11
           IF `the Court has extended the time` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 23(3), (4)(b)
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 23(3) -- the application must be made within 6 months from the grant` `the six months`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `apply to the Supreme Court under s 23` `the day I apply it`))
               WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST TRUE
               OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- s 23(3) "must be made within 6 months"
    WHERE
        `the six months` MEANS `the days from` `the day probate or administration was granted or resealed in NSW` `to` (`the last day to apply under s 23` `the day probate or administration was granted or resealed in NSW`)
        `the offset` MEANS `the days from` `the day probate or administration was granted or resealed in NSW` `to` `the day I apply`
        `the day I apply it` MEANS IF `the offset` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the offset`

§§ `ss 4, 27, 33, 35, 36 -- asking a tribunal to review the power (a situation: you are worried about the power or the attorney)`

@export Can I apply to NCAT or the Supreme Court for a review of someone's power of attorney (ss 4, 27, 33, 35, 36)?
GIVEN `my place in relation to the power` IS A `My place in relation to the power` @desc Your position. One of: "I am an attorney under the power, even if my appointment was purportedly revoked", "I am the principal", "I am the principal's guardian", "I am the principal's enduring guardian", "someone else, such as a relative or friend"
      `the tribunal finds I have a proper interest or genuine concern` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are "someone else, such as a relative or friend", whether the tribunal is of the opinion that you have a proper interest in the proceedings or a genuine concern for the principal's welfare
      `the tribunal I would apply to` IS A `The review tribunal` @desc Where you would apply. One of: "the Civil and Administrative Tribunal" (NCAT), "the Supreme Court"
      `the power is an enduring power of attorney` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the power is an enduring power of attorney (or an older "protected" power under the Conveyancing Act 1919); a review of its revocation counts too
      `an incapacity makes the principal unable to understand communications about their property or affairs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a physical or mental incapacity (temporary or permanent, including one caused by a drug or treatment) makes the principal unable to understand communications about their property or affairs
      `an incapacity makes the principal unable to express their intentions about their property or affairs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether such an incapacity makes the principal unable to express their intentions about their property or affairs
      `the principal cannot be located or contacted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the principal cannot be located or contacted
      `I already applied to the other tribunal about the same matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied to the other review tribunal for the same thing about the same (or substantially the same) matter
      `that application was withdrawn with approval` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that earlier application was withdrawn with that tribunal's approval
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for a review of the power of attorney` MEANS
    `may apply for a review` `my place in relation to the power` `the tribunal finds I have a proper interest or genuine concern` `the tribunal I would apply to` `the power is an enduring power of attorney` (`the principal is incommunicate` `an incapacity makes the principal unable to understand communications about their property or affairs` `an incapacity makes the principal unable to express their intentions about their property or affairs` `the principal cannot be located or contacted`) `I already applied to the other tribunal about the same matter` `that application was withdrawn with approval`

-- s 36(3) "A review tribunal may make ... (b) an order declaring that the power of attorney is invalid (either in whole or
-- in part) if the tribunal is satisfied (i) the principal did not have the capacity necessary to make it, or (ii) the
-- power of attorney did not comply with the other requirements of this Act applicable to it, or (iii) the power of
-- attorney is invalid for any other reason, for example, the principal was induced to make it by dishonesty or undue
-- influence." The tribunal's satisfaction is asked.
@export On a review, can the tribunal declare the power of attorney invalid (s 36(3)(b))?
GIVEN `the tribunal is satisfied the principal lacked capacity to make it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied the principal did not have the capacity needed to make the power
      `the tribunal is satisfied it did not comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied the power did not comply with the Act's other requirements (for example the s 19 witness and certificate)
      `the tribunal is satisfied it is invalid for another reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied it is invalid for another reason, for example the principal was induced to make it by dishonesty or undue influence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the tribunal declare the power invalid` MEANS
    IF    `the tribunal is satisfied the principal lacked capacity to make it`
       OR `the tribunal is satisfied it did not comply with the Act`
       OR `the tribunal is satisfied it is invalid for another reason`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 36(3)(b) "if the tribunal is satisfied"

-- s 36(4) "A review tribunal may, if satisfied that it would be in the best interests of the principal to do so or that
-- it would better reflect the wishes of the principal, make any one or more of the following orders": (a) varying a
-- term or power, (b) removing an attorney, (c) appointing a substitute, (d) reinstating a lapsed power, (e) accounts,
-- records, audit, a financial management plan, (f) revoking all or part of the power, (g) other orders. (12) "This
-- section has effect subject to the provisions of Part 3 (Irrevocable powers of attorney)."
@export On a review, can the tribunal remove the attorney, order accounts, or vary or revoke the power (s 36(4))?
GIVEN `the tribunal is satisfied it is in the principal's best interests` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied the order would be in the principal's best interests
      `the tribunal is satisfied it better reflects the principal's wishes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied the order would better reflect the principal's wishes
      `the power is irrevocable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the power is irrevocable (expressed to be irrevocable and given for valuable consideration, s 15)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the tribunal remove the attorney or change the power` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the power is irrevocable` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 36(12) "subject to" Part 3, which does not say how; fork F12)
           IF `the tribunal is satisfied it is in the principal's best interests` OR `the tribunal is satisfied it better reflects the principal's wishes` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- s 36(4) "if satisfied"
